well matt hancock was telling us earlier  about the pressure on the nhs and of  course it's something that's been felt  by those working on the front line  including paramedics and those people  driving ambulances well tracy nichols is  the chief executive of the college of  paramedics and she joins us now  how much pressure is the ambulance  service under  uh it's under unprecedented pressure um  sophie we're very used to seeing  ambulance services  um take some strain over the winter  months due to  the normal pressures that you'd see any  particular year  but but this year particularly has seen  incredible pressure  because of uh the clinical presentation  of the patients that  the our members are seeing um they are  sicker  and and we are seeing this incredible  bottleneck  despite the herculean efforts of both  our paramedics and ambulance staff and  the  emergency department teams we're seeing  the ambulance handover delays  at a scale we haven't seen before we've  all seen of course  those images of ambulances backed up  outside hospitals waiting  to get the patients inside how long are  ambulances having to wait outside  hospitals and is that putting patient  safety at risk  so i think it's different across the  country  and we've seen certainly in london and  the southeast an increased level of  pressure  recently our members have reported to us  that they can wait as little as half an  hour  uh we've had some members who've waited  five six seven eight and even nine hours  nine hours nine hours and that's with a  patient for their experience we know  that patient experience  if that's poor that can affect clinical  outcomes  but i would say sophie the hidden risk  is that you can your your  your viewers can see the ambulances at  the hospitals that doesn't take into  account the huge number of patients that  are waiting for an ambulance  that can't get to them so the control  room staff who are also doing a  fantastic job are holding those calls  until they can get a response it's very  true the sort of hidden  delays how long are people having to  wait for ambulances  so for the immediate category one calls  the life-threatening calls there doesn't  appear to be  too many delays for category two calls  we have seen in those high pressure  areas around  london in the south east slight delays  of maybe an additional 10 minutes  is really those category three and four  calls those category three calls would  be things like abdominal pains or falls  and some of those patients in those high  pressure areas have waited up to 10  hours  up to 10 hours for an ambulance and i  guess as you say you know these are  category 3 and 4  it's not deemed to be life-threatening  but at the same time people need  ambulances for these things  that's the reason that they're calling  them right absolutely and and you know  we we are  cognizant of the fact that our members  are working in an environment  particularly in this weather where  if someone falls outside for example the  people in the call center will try their  absolute utmost to try and get a  response to somebody  um but it's it's really frightening when  you're waiting for an ambulance even if  it arrives in in minutes  it's a scary thing for a patient to wait  for an ambulance  it's worth talking as well isn't it that  you know covert is having an impact on  people who don't  have covert as well who have other  health problems  who need ambulances i'm thinking of  anyone from you know someone having a  stroke to someone having a fall to  someone  even having a home birth for example and  then needing potentially to go to  hospital  is there a risk that if the nhs  continues to face the kind of pressure  it's under now cases are still rising  that you might not be able to get an  ambulance that you need  because of the pressure i think it is a  potential risk  and you know we are open for business as  is all the nhs the  the ambulance services are doing an  amazing job under difficult  circumstances  and we really want people who who have  those conditions to call the stroke  networks are still open  the heart attack centers are still open  the trauma networks are still open  um we really do need people to  understand that  um kobe doesn't preclude them from  getting help and can  but but i'm sure it's frightening for  people to to think that  um because of the pressures from kobe  that they may  somehow not be able to get an ambulance  in the same way  when i was researching this interview  read some reports um  mainly from kind of local newspapers  around london saying that you know some  people are having to be transported  in police cars or even fire engines and  because they can't  get the ambulance as they need is that  happening um  we we know of rare occasions where that  does and  and certainly i know london ambulance  service have engaged with  london fire brigade and and the met  police to  to try and um spread the load of of  paramedics and clinicians  um across the the capital so that uh the  ambulances can get there so people  aren't conveyed in a  in a less safe environment uh such as an  ambulance but  um you know that does bring with it its  own risk so i understand from our other  emergency service colleagues that if  you're waiting on scene  with someone who you think is ill you  haven't been able to necessarily  clinically assess them in the same way  you're going to do whatever you think is  right  just finally we've known for quite a  long time that  winter peak of covert 19 was likely  to happen and bringing with it increased  pressure we know the nhs has always  under increased pressure in winter  anyway  and yet here we are you know not enough  staff not enough beds  ambulances backed up against outside  hospitals sometimes  up to nine hours what were the  contingency plans drawn up were you  involved in these contingency plans  uh the college wasn't but that's not  necessarily a surprise the  the ambulance services were there there  are you know it's part of the critical  infrastructure  of the country so uh they absolutely  were but i think  what what wasn't anticipated you know  the new variant has  has ripped through uh the population and  um you've got staff who are uh are you  know  really trying hard to come in even if  they're not feeling great themselves  not with kovid you know if they test uh  positive for covid they're doing the  right thing  um but they're frightened that they're  gonna introduce infection they work  predominantly in the communities but  certainly i think this has superseded  all the plans that we we could have had  but uh we do need to um support  the paramedic and ambulance workforce  with with good protection so they  protect the patients that's what  everybody wants to do  and we really need to work together to  try and stem this bottleneck that's  happening at the emergency departments  thank you you've spoken really  powerfully about the you know pressure  that your staff  and the ambulances are under so we  really appreciate you coming on thank  you  thank you sophie

